PMAT-534 — the latest release is the latest release
===================================================

### 1. what /releases/latest resolves to now, and the history behind it
  repos/paiml/forjar/releases/latest -> v1.29.0   (created 2026-09-11T19:55:10Z)

  the eight most recent release objects:
    v1.29.0	prerelease=false	draft=false	created=2026-09-11T19:55:10Z
    nightly	prerelease=true	draft=false	created=2026-09-11T07:46:08Z
    v1.28.0	prerelease=false	draft=false	created=2026-09-10T16:07:14Z
    v1.27.0	prerelease=false	draft=false	created=2026-09-08T22:10:01Z
    v1.26.0	prerelease=false	draft=false	created=2026-09-08T12:02:13Z
    v1.25.2	prerelease=false	draft=false	created=2026-09-05T16:25:29Z
    v1.25.1	prerelease=false	draft=false	created=2026-09-04T02:07:29Z
    v1.25.0	prerelease=false	draft=false	created=2026-09-04T00:13:32Z

  It points at v1.29.0 today because it was corrected BY HAND during this
  session. Before that it resolved to v1.25.2 while v1.26.0, v1.27.0 and
  v1.28.0 were all prerelease=false draft=false and days newer -- the state
  PMAT-534 was filed from.

  The `nightly` row is the case the arm must NOT refuse: a deliberate rolling
  prerelease, which GitHub will never make latest.
### 2. why clearing the flag is not enough
  A release is born a DRAFT PRERELEASE (.github/workflows/release.yml, PMAT-166)
  so no consumer sees a half-uploaded asset set, and the publish job un-drafts it
  while LEAVING it a prerelease:
    548:              gh release edit "$RELEASE_TAG" --draft=false --prerelease --repo "${{ github.repository }}"
  GitHub fixes "latest" when the prerelease flag is WRITTEN and never recomputes
  it, so a later `gh release edit <tag> --prerelease=false` clears the flag and
  leaves the pointer where it was. The promotion must say `--latest`.

